# Every object that `state` holds is written out with a whole number in each of its four fields, so a read of one is a load and the sum stays on integers.
# `other` never meets `state`. But a record is one layout for each set of names, and the literal that begins with a spread is born as that layout with fields that may be
# absent: the fields of the layout are then undefined | int for every reader, the loop of `written` asks each of its three reads whether it found anything, and the sum runs on
# numbers: 51 instructions a turn alone, 88 beside the spread. Where the spread has names of its own nothing of that happens.
# TODO: Link: a literal that begins with a spread shares the layout, and what is proven of its fields, with the record that is written out under the same names
# f1 = written
# RUN: walk f1 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-NOT: GetSlotOrUndefined
# CHECK: = GetSlot {{*}}key="b" receivers=[L{{*}}] level=Proven
